      ":question/id": "0e124a40-e99e-5182-a65e-8b1700ec7c72",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 19 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -50 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{12}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}+R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 19 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{3}:=R_{3}-4R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 19 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -50 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{2}:=R_{2}+R_{1}$ and $R_{3}:=R_{3}-4R_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
